# Spring Boot / Java Backend Rules

These rules are MANDATORY when writing or editing code in this project.

## 1. Think Before Coding
State assumptions explicitly. If multiple interpretations exist, present them — don't pick silently. If unclear, ask.

## 2. Simplicity First
Minimum code that solves the problem. No speculative abstractions, configurability, or error handling for impossible scenarios. If 200 lines could be 50, rewrite.

## 3. Surgical Changes
Touch only what the user asked for. Don't improve adjacent code, comments, or formatting. Match existing style.

## 4. Goal-Driven Execution
Every task gets verifiable success criteria. State a brief plan with verification checks before multi-step work.

## 5. No Hardcoding
Never hardcode config values, error keys, or business literals. Extract to a Constants class, config file, or Enum. Magic strings/numbers are bugs waiting to happen.

## 6. Thin Controllers, Fat Services
Controllers ONLY: receive request → validate → call service → return response. Zero business logic, transformations, or DB queries in controllers. If a controller method has `if-else` business decisions, move it to a service.

## 7. SOLID & Clean Code
Strictly enforce SOLID — especially Single Responsibility and Dependency Inversion. If new code is hard to unit test, refactor.

## 8. MapStruct for All Mapping
Never write manual `dto.setX(entity.getX())`. Every Entity ↔ DTO ↔ Domain conversion goes through a `@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")` MapStruct interface.

## 9. Lombok for Boilerplate
Use `@Getter`, `@Setter`, `@RequiredArgsConstructor`, `@Builder`, `@NoArgsConstructor`, `@AllArgsConstructor`. Never hand-write getters, setters, or standard constructors.

## 10. UUID v7 for All Entity IDs
Every entity primary key uses UUID v7 (time-ordered, index-friendly). Use a library like `com.github.f4b6a3:uuid-creator` and generate in `@PrePersist`.

## 11. Proactive Security & Performance Audits
While reading or writing code, if you detect:
- SQL injection risk → STOP, report, propose parameterized fix
- Broken access control → STOP, report
- N+1 query → STOP, suggest `@EntityGraph` or `JOIN FETCH`
- Heavy loops, missing indexes, blocking calls → flag immediately

Never silently ship architectural risk.

## 12. Custom `PageResponse<T>` Wrapper
Paginated endpoints MUST return `PageResponse<T>`, never Spring's raw `Page<T>`. Maintains API stability when paging shape evolves.

## 13. Localized Error Messages
All user-facing error messages (Exception messages, `@ResponseStatus` responses, validation messages) MUST be in the end-user's language. English is for logs only.

> **Customize for your audience.** This file ships with Vietnamese as the default (the original author's target market). Replace with your audience's language, or delete this rule if your end-users read English natively.

Examples (Vietnamese default):
- ✅ `"Email này đã được sử dụng. Vui lòng dùng email khác."`
- ❌ `"Email already exists"` (when shipping to non-English users)

## 14. Roleplay User & Edge Cases
Before declaring a feature "done," explicitly list at least 2 user-error scenarios (e.g., huge file upload, malformed input, race conditions) and confirm the code handles them. No "happy path only."

## 15. Two-Tier Validation (Declarative + Strategy)
Split validation into two tiers:
- **Tier 1 — Stateless** (null, blank, regex, size, range, enum, static cross-field like `endDate` after `startDate`): `jakarta.validation` annotations on request DTOs, triggered by `@Valid`/`@Validated` in the controller. Never re-check in the service.
- **Tier 2 — Stateful/business** (uniqueness, balance, inventory, ownership, status transitions — needs a DB lookup or another entity): keep out of the controller. NEVER inject a `Repository` into a `ConstraintValidator` — it hides rules, runs outside `@Transactional`, and invites N+1/session bugs.

For business rules, model each as a `@Component` implementing a shared `XxxValidator` interface, inject `List<XxxValidator>` into the service, and loop — Spring auto-collects every implementation, so adding a rule means adding a class (OCP), not editing the service. Control run order with `@Order`/`Ordered`; use a Chain of Responsibility for strict pass-then-proceed gating.

---

When in doubt, re-read these rules. They prevent the exact failure modes that ship to production.
